WS73 hardware-free USB regression
================================

Run from a clean committed kernel worktree on an x86-64 Linux host with
Python 3, GCC/glibc, Clang/LLD, make, QEMU and permission to use KVM.
Reserve four build jobs and two VM CPUs within the shared machine budget.
These scripts do not install a kernel or load a host module. Never execute
the compiled init fixture directly on the host or add physical USB passthrough.

  python3 tools/testing/ws73/usb-vm/build.py
  python3 tools/testing/ws73/usb-vm/run.py --speed full
  python3 tools/testing/ws73/usb-vm/run.py --speed high
  python3 tools/testing/ws73/usb-vm/run.py --phase boot --speed full
  python3 tools/testing/ws73/usb-vm/run.py --phase boot --speed high
  python3 tools/testing/ws73/usb-vm/run.py --phase download --speed full
  python3 tools/testing/ws73/usb-vm/run.py --phase download --speed high
  python3 tools/testing/ws73/usb-vm/run.py --phase loader --speed full --firmware-root /path/to/ws73_SDK/firmware
  python3 tools/testing/ws73/usb-vm/run.py --phase loader --speed high --firmware-root /path/to/ws73_SDK/firmware
  python3 tools/testing/ws73/usb-vm/run.py --phase startup --speed full
  python3 tools/testing/ws73/usb-vm/run.py --phase startup --speed high
  python3 tools/testing/ws73/usb-vm/run.py --phase hci --speed full
  python3 tools/testing/ws73/usb-vm/run.py --phase hci --speed high
  python3 tools/testing/ws73/usb-vm/check-supervisor.py <full-speed-record>

Do not use python -O: assertions form part of the verification and optimized
execution is explicitly rejected. The kernel is built from allnoconfig plus
usb.config; every requested configuration line must be satisfied. No earlier
test archive or private baseline configuration is needed. The build output is
fixed at ../.dev/ws73-upstream/build-virtual-usb relative to the source tree;
a shared build.lock excludes the other WS73 build/test scripts. Observation
hardware kernels in build-observe are not touched. The accepted build record
pointer and timestamped artifacts live under ../.dev/ws73-upstream.

The runner requires the build's clean source commit to match the current
worktree, including tools and documentation. Rebuild after committing changes.
It checks the exact required artifact set and hashes before starting QEMU,
including the BLE endpoint, runtime-link, shared USB IO, boot-loader and
download-test and native HCI objects. Only the explicit HCI phase publishes
a BLE child; building the objects alone does not exercise that path.
ACPI and PCI are required for this Q35 guest to power off: ACPI's default
PCI_Config region handler depends on PCI. Guest shutdown must terminate QEMU
successfully; a fixture PASS line followed by a process timeout is a failure.
This shutdown configuration is not WS73 device PM qualification.

The VM has no host USB, disk or network connection. It uses FunctionFS and
dummy_hcd to enumerate synthetic runtime devices, with separate kernel and
fixture serial logs. Each speed exercises 17 cases covering identity/version,
notifications, IRQ faults, injected disconnect and callback drain. A process
timeout kills only the VM started by the runner. Source, configuration,
tool, runtime library, kernel and fixture hashes accompany each attempt.
The negative checker corrupts a verified full-speed log in 32 ways and checks
five invalid build manifests (stale source, failed build, missing/extra artifact
and wrong hash). Every corruption must be rejected. It does not exercise a
device. Run it once for each phase's full-speed PASS record. Boot mode checks
27 log corruptions and the same five invalid build manifests. Download mode
checks 28 log corruptions and five invalid build manifests. Loader mode checks
33 log corruptions, five build artifacts and four firmware provenance/hash
corruptions. The six original files and the rehashed negative input are verified
against their recorded identities before accepting a loader result.

The boot phase enumerates two-endpoint synthetic devices in 14 cases.
Each successful identity exchange is followed by the exact eight-byte VERSION
query and a three-byte or full 64-byte opaque reply, independently checked
against the driver observation. The full-packet cases send no extra zero packet. There are seven such exchanges; failed identity never sends
VERSION. This is a synthetic ROM reply, not firmware compatibility evidence. It checks
the exact fixed READM command, U/USB (including zero), E/USB, S/SDIO and E/UART
observations, replies of 0/1/3/5/512 bytes, a withheld reply timeout, removal
while awaiting a reply and fresh U/E instances after removal. Decoding S/SDIO
or E/UART does not enable those transports. Thirteen cases also check that no
extra OUT command arrives during a 100-ms window before removal. FunctionFS
synchronous reads are interrupted and drained by a periodic signal timer;
O_NONBLOCK alone does not bound queued IO. These are live USB transfers in
dummy_hcd, not proof that a real cold chip exposes the strap register.

The download phase uses the separate synthetic ID ffff:3734 and a test-only
USB driver that also requires the dummy_hcd controller. It calls the production
FILES/QUIT executor and shared URB/READY-wait helpers with explicit synthetic
views of 32769/32/32 bytes. The peer independently checks commands, all payload
bytes and QUIT. Seven cases per speed cover success, bad READY, bad FILES OK,
timeout, removal at READY/payload admission, then a fresh successful instance.
Five cases check no extra OUT for 100 ms; every instance must report callback
drain. Successful QUIT means WAIT_RUNTIME only. No runtime instance is started.

The fixture reads the actual endpoint descriptor and sizes its first payload
read to the negotiated 64/512-byte packet. A smaller high-speed read can
truncate the transfer and overflow FunctionFS. Wire timing includes scheduling;
the test driver separately measures the actual shared wait call and the
supervisor requires all ten waits to be at least 5000000 ns. The production
parent's boot transfer limit remains 32768 bytes, with no runtime ZLP flag.

This phase does not request original firmware, validate hashes, perform RAM/XO
preparation or prove device identity. Those contracts have separate tests;
this synthetic executor is not an alternative physical loading path. These
The runtime/boot/download phases do not execute armed startup/customization, BLE class-control, HCI
or Wi-Fi, nor qualify physical U/E, RF, PM or peers. Missing resources, failed
configuration dependencies, malformed logs and unexpected driver records fail
the run rather than broadening the accepted result.

The loader phase additionally needs authorized local SDK110 firmware/us and
firmware/e originals. It pins all six files using check-firmware.py before
building the private initramfs; the code payload mutation is generated locally
and given a valid recomputed internal hash. Original and negative files are
copied only into local test records/initramfs, never into the kernel repository.
Do not publish those binary records as part of a patch series. The documented
hashes and source code are sufficient to identify the inputs.

This phase uses ffff:3735 on dummy_hcd to call ws73_boot_start. Ten cases check
fresh READM selection, actual request_firmware_direct, all-input validation,
11 fixed RAM/XO writes, FILES payloads and QUIT. Cases include U/E success,
missing BTC, rehashed modification, mixed calibration, S/SDIO and E/UART
rejection, identity-stage removal, bad WRITEM reply and fresh U success.
Repeating a finished call must return the first error or -EALREADY without IO.
Nine no-extra-OUT windows and all ten disconnect drains are required. The peer
checks 22 original payload chunks per speed; it does not execute that code.

Board trim 1/2 is an explicit synthetic value. This does not qualify real
board registers, cold identity, hardware firmware execution, re-enumeration or
runtime identity agreement. Original-file rejection is exercised through the
kernel API, but cancellation/latency during filesystem acquisition itself is
not fault-injected. Missing local runner inputs produce a FAIL manifest before
QEMU starts; they cannot be interpreted as a skipped or successful hardware run.

Runtime startup / USB action integration
---------------------------------------
Run `python3 tools/testing/ws73/usb-vm/run.py --phase startup --speed full`
(and `--speed high`) after building this revision. The ffff:3736 test
consumer binds only dummy_hcd and reuses the production USB owner's
allocation, RX/interrupt callbacks, control transfers and disconnect drain.
Seven cases cover startup with early BOOT and acknowledged BLE disable,
missing enable reply, ep0 STALL, malformed customization acknowledgment,
removal while waiting for enable, simultaneous BSP/panic, and a fresh
successful instance. Check exact bulk bytes, class SETUP/data, independent
q10 replies, terminal failure, and absence of further bulk/class OUT.
The all-zero customization is an explicit fixture, not a physical board
profile. This test registers no HCI controller and supplies no hardware,
firmware execution, BLE peer or RF qualification. Keep physical observations
and actual function qualification separate. The physical USB ID still uses
the observation worker; no startup test parameter enables hardware loading.

Native HCI over USB
------------------
The `--phase hci` fixture uses ffff:3737 (dummy_hcd only) to publish the
actual auxiliary endpoint after the acknowledged startup/disable sequence.
The native BT_WS73 consumer then enables BLE, performs setup and HCI init
through real USB HCC transfers, and closes through the same action backend.
The explicit userspace peer supplies synthetic LE-only identity, no optional
command bits, no optional LE features and no RF behavior. It is not a model
of WS73 controller capabilities or Bluetooth conformance. The standard HCI
management channel must announce a configured index and return the fixture
address/version/LE-only identity. A separate case removes USB while Reset
is pending, followed by a fresh successful instance. These tests supplement
physical boot observations; they do not qualify physical HCI, BlueZ scanning,
connections, L2CAP, security, ACL traffic or a real peer.

After each configured-controller disconnect, keep its management socket open
until one correctly sized Index Removed for index zero is received. Allow
queued New Settings, reject other records, and check a further 100 ms without
events before closing the socket. Missing, duplicate, short, wrong-index and
premature removal fail supervision. Reset-in-flight cancellation never reached
a configured index, so it does not require the configured-removal event.

The startup and HCI supervisors reject respectively 29 and 41 log mutations,
each with five artifact mutations. All-module compilation also includes the
startup/HCI test consumer and its USB-owner dependency. Bluetooth is enabled
in the virtual fixture configuration; it has no NIC or physical USB connection.

Pending Reset cancellation must drain within one second of the kernel USB
disconnect timestamp (below HCI_CMD_TIMEOUT of two seconds), without an HCI
command timeout in the kernel log. Late-drain and timeout negative controls
are rejected explicitly; teardown completion alone is insufficient.

The startup/HCI fixture now requires a complete 128-byte platform PM USB
message after identity/version and BSP, before customization. Its PM word is
an explicit zero fixture (sleep disabled, no physical wiring claim). Startup
has 52 bulk OUT per speed; HCI has 57. The PM message has no synthetic ACK.
Missing, short and reordered PM records fail supervision. These counts and
byte checks do not establish physical device PM behavior.
